#{[17592186372064 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372064 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372064 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "aeb916fe-deac-5c6b-a3af-a1e29579a5af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372065 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372065 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372065 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ee658281-8425-5be1-8788-54102c3c6175" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372066 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372066 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372066 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "660c3c54-a204-504f-ae3d-31a70939bfb6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372067 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372067 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372067 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "160f71b3-9073-5c2f-99f9-acde1bf550a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372068 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372068 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372068 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fd0c4912-d2bd-5e3f-9221-2060507e5033" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372076 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372076 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162520" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372076 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372076 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372076 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "622f220e-fb66-51d7-b3ac-edc3a1b8924a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372076 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ and $Q(x,y)=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a no stream function, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=(1-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\ne 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372077 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372077 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372077 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372077 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372077 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372077 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372077 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372077 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372077 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "3cada9ed-c85a-58fb-a254-1769ba4f3d5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372078 #edb/ref 1080 "$F$ has no stream function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372078 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372078 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "67b8f6d6-9607-5514-abbd-603cb08ece42" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372079 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372079 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372079 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "72008387-8f3a-5d1d-965c-282a4ef1a635" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372080 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372080 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372080 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7b24f282-d62b-5e80-a78c-10312ff8606e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372081 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372081 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372081 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7a7e266d-e3fe-59e3-b2ef-457c0782331f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372082 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)=-xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372082 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372082 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "be1595e1-671d-5e1a-95b7-d83ff63e6f83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372083 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162522" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372083 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372083 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,j?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5fa02a5c-65fb-5f22-a3ed-8e6c445f1c62" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372083 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=2xy$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a no stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2y+2y=4y\\ne 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372084 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372084 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372084 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372087 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372084 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372084 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372084 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372084 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372084 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372084 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "126114a5-a04b-5b6e-a5e7-1561c7926bd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372085 #edb/ref 1080 "$F$ has no stream function" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372085 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372085 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e32f2c3b-fa9c-5bf6-a35b-57839d0e21d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372086 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+2xy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372086 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372086 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "703dbab9-9c69-5646-a97b-dd8ef0a32c99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372087 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372087 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372087 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b03ae81f-442d-5398-a1d0-eb48182ed670" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372088 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+xy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372088 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372088 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06d4c7ba-315f-5ed3-b710-82a77b517287" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372089 #edb/ref 1080 "ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372089 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372089 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ffb65d35-dc25-51cf-95b6-281265b1d418" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472074 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472075 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472076 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162608" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n- **I.** $F(x,y)=3x\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **II.** $F(x,y)=x\\,i+x{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **III.** $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-2xy\\,j$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f67f3f6-b8ed-585a-bae8-a685dca4c830" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372153 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=3+(-2y)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+2xy\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2x+(-2x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472077 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472078 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472079 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n- **I.** $F(x,y)=4{y}^{2}x\\,i-x{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- **II.** $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$\n- **III.** $F(x,y)=2yx\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "169dc9ad-6608-5ab8-8ec5-7973ca027bd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372160 #edb/ref 1074 "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each vector field.\n\n- Vector field I doesn't have a stream function. The partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=4{y}^{2}+(-2xy)\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III has a stream function. Indeed, the partial derivatives are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2y+(-2y)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372181 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372181 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162619" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372181 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372181 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the equation of the streamline that passes through the point $P(0,\\pi )$ for the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372181 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd6e61d1-6465-500e-9577-5af9c54bc069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372181 #edb/ref 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n. \n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curve of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ that passes through $P.$\nFirst, we find the value of the stream function at $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(0,\\pi ) & ={e}^{(0)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the streamline that passes through $P(0,\\pi )$ is the $0$-level curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our possible streamlines are $y=k\\pi $ for any integer $k.$ The streamline that passes through $P(0,\\pi )$ is $y=\\pi .$\nThe vector field and the streamline through the point $P$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/bd/bd732367a876d2e6bba6bf96728ebcf8430176d2f480bff30a054cac5389b1f2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372187 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372182 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1626cb5c-1d71-5bed-88ae-3afa894a0595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372183 #edb/ref 1080 "x=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372183 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372183 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3041c7b2-ad5d-59e4-a35f-cdd3f5a8ded4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372184 #edb/ref 1080 "y=\\pi {e}^{x}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372184 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372184 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8e0bfc27-933c-52a6-a9da-b121d5422481" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372185 #edb/ref 1080 "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372185 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372185 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4b692407-30c1-5d1f-ac8b-4238f37dea05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372186 #edb/ref 1080 "x=\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372186 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372186 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1dbbfc69-0b08-5509-a6ec-62eeb283eb76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372187 #edb/ref 1080 "y=\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372187 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372187 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2d8466d6-4bb0-570a-a74f-a68de142e108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186389340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=2xi-2y\\,j$ over the domain $D={R}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b93df85-0b3c-5ded-8201-a97d95285f56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389339 #edb/ref 1074 "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2xi-2y\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=2x$ and $Q(x,y)=-2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2-2=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=2xy+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=2xy$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ 2xy & =c \\\\ y & =\\frac{c}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=0$ and $x=0$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=0$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/24/24c6dadd4f980121a36929a9cdb800f7da8fac8eb84af40d595af89aa77b11ea.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186389345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186389344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389340 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ce55e1ed-b422-5201-909f-65fba9887b50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389341 #edb/ref 1080 "images/6a/6a1ce9b96f0bca4ec14e7ce60e5cebd72efc4421acfd4a65ba55e9c8a7d1d352.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186389341 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17